        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_24-2.lnote.de.3" n="3" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">206</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">nicht ‘to keep possession’.—Selbstgezüchtetes tritt allgemein in Ggs. zu Gekauftem, heisst <hi rend="italic">unbeboht</hi>; Ælfred <hi rend="italic">Oros</hi>. ed. Sweet 18</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">not ‘to keep possession’.—What one has bred oneself is generally contrasted with what has been bought, and is called <hi rend="italic">unbeboht</hi>; Ælfred <hi rend="italic">Oros</hi>. ed. Sweet 18</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="IICn_24-2.robnote.n.1" n="1" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">355</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">It is difficult to understand the exact meaning of <hi rend="italic">ahnian</hi> here and in the following clause; cf. however Leis Wl. 45, 2 f. The difficulty with regard to § 3 is increased by the peculiar use of <hi rend="italic">sculan</hi>, perhaps due to its being a negative sentence.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="IICn_24-2.robnote.fn.6" n="6" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">186</biblScope></bibl><label>witnysse</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">gewitnesse</hi> G; <hi rend="italic">-witnysse</hi> A.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="IICn_24-2.robnote.fn.7" n="7" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">186</biblScope></bibl><label>cwædon</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">s. w. her beforan cw.</hi> G; <hi rend="italic">swa hær b. cw. is</hi> A, Ld.</seg></note>
